00:51:32,720 --> 00:51:39,720
 Well, yeah, I'll let Darren clarify a little bit, but you'll notice there's an unadjusted number and then an adjusted number, right?

336
00:51:39,720 --> 00:51:57,720
 So here's one of these nuanced sort of seeming paradoxes that you need to kind of be aware of when you see these things is when it costs less than a penny to move a million, ten million, even a hundred million dollars, then it happens a lot.

337
00:51:57,720 --> 00:52:03,720
 Right, because people are rebalancing their internal institutions or rebalancing their portfolios.

338
00:52:03,720 --> 00:52:06,720
 Exchanges are recycling through different wallets, right?

339
00:52:06,720 --> 00:52:09,720
 All these things happen because they can happen cheaply, right?

340
00:52:09,720 --> 00:52:21,720
 Think about if electricity in your house costs like $10,000 per kilowatt hour, you would be incredibly sparing and careful with like every single thing that you do.

341
00:52:21,720 --> 00:52:27,720
 If energy costs less than a penny per kilowatt hour, then you don't give a shit at all.

342
00:52:27,720 --> 00:52:33,720
 Like, you leave the lights on, you know, like blow dryer ever as long as you want, crank the water heater up.

343
00:52:33,720 --> 00:52:41,720
 In other words, the apparently low cost affects these numbers for what may seem like large behaviors to people.

344
00:52:41,720 --> 00:52:46,720
 To normal people, and this is reasonable, you wouldn't move around a million dollars willy nilly.

345
00:52:46,720 --> 00:52:50,720
 But if it costs you next to nothing, then maybe you actually do.

346
00:52:50,720 --> 00:53:00,720
 So, one of the interesting consequences of extremely low gas costs, mature infrastructure, institutional adoption, et cetera, and stable coin proliferation

