01:01:00,720 --> 01:01:14,720
 Tell us about what's going on in the privacy world. A theme whenever I talk on the pod, I feel like some of my A16 Z neurons that have grown in my brain have a very specific shape.

414
01:01:14,720 --> 01:01:31,720
 The shape is that technology develops in this sort of historical way. The best way to predict the future, even though predicting is flawed, is to kind of unfold technology like one layer at a time and try to imagine what happens.

415
01:01:31,720 --> 01:01:44,720
 What bottlenecks appear, which second-order consequences occur counterintuitively. That's the way to think about it. Privacy could not be more obvious to me.

416
01:01:44,720 --> 01:01:55,720
 It has been for years. I will scream it forever. I don't care if this uptick in privacy is a fad and it goes away. It's going to come back again later. It is inevitable.

417
01:01:55,720 --> 01:02:12,720
 The reason no one has cared is because if stuff doesn't work, if no one's using it, you don't need it to be private. At the same time, things being illegible and confusing is often a workable substitute for privacy in the short term.

418
01:02:12,720 --> 01:02:29,720
 Crypto has plenty of this illegibility and confusingness. Combine a lack of total mainstream adoption for consumer use cases like payments, which we've been saying may happen, has the signs of happening isn't happening yet.

