01:02:29,720 --> 01:02:46,720
 Consumers don't make their payments with stable coins, but for their to-be payments of stable coins, you need stable coins and you need stable coin adoption and you need institutional acceptance of stable coins and you need transaction fees to be low, etc. All these other things that we've talked about.

420
01:02:46,720 --> 01:02:59,720
 Once that becomes the case, people will demand privacy. I know this is like a hotly... This is talked about in crypto all the time. Ad nauseam makes me crazy. People say people don't care about privacy, whatever.

421
01:02:59,720 --> 01:03:15,720
 No, of course they care about privacy. They do care about... They won't necessarily articulate it in this way, but if someone comes up and shows you Venmo from 10 years ago, someone shows you all the transactions you've paid and all the comments and it's all out in the wild, people have an adverse reaction.

422
01:03:15,720 --> 01:03:23,720
 People take it for granted. That's what they mean by don't care is that they take it for granted. They just assume they have a functional level of privacy.

423
01:03:23,720 --> 01:03:33,720
 They don't demand specific cryptography. They don't demand like incredible definitions about the distinction between privacy, anonymity, pseudo-nimity, confidentiality.

424
01:03:33,720 --> 01:03:37,720
 They don't do that, but they have expectations.

425
01:03:37,720 --> 01:03:48,720
 Once these things become popular tools, blockchains for payments, blockchains for personal finance, blockchains, neobanks, whatever, then people will demand privacy.

426
01:03:48,720 --> 01:04:01,720
 And institutions will demand privacy. A very clear theme for me over the last year or so as I've begun to have more conversations and institutions is that they consider privacy a non-negotiable property.

427
01:04:01,720 --> 01:04:11,720
 It is a table stakes property they must have. They cannot reveal their behavior to competitors, to the public. They cannot make the public uneasy.

428
01:04:11,720 --> 01:04:16,720
 They need to be able to offer this to their customers. They've made it abundantly clear to me.

429
01:04:16,720 --> 01:04:24,720
 You might say, "Why don't we have it yet in crypto?" There's two reasons. One is that the ways crypto has been popular so far hasn't really needed it.

430
01:04:24,720 --> 01:04:33,720
 If you're just moving a little bit out of an exchange, you're putting it in DeFi, you're just messing around with some meme coins, you're in an emerging economy, you want to hold some dollars.

431
01:04:33,720 --> 01:04:40,720
 These don't really demand a profound privacy, and the illegibility of crypto might be good enough for you.

432
01:04:40,720 --> 01:04:53,720
 But the minute it starts to get into more private things, salary, payments, personal finance, then people will start to do contrived things to get privacy.

433
01:04:53,720 --> 01:04:58,720
 They'll start to make tons of addresses. They'll start to use apps that offer. It's just absolutely going to happen.

434
01:04:58,720 --> 01:05:12,720
 The second is that privacy is very difficult technologically and from a compliance perspective. Technologically, we've needed a lot of advancements across different areas in cryptography to try to get closer to privacy.

