630
01:29:33,720 --> 01:29:34,720
 Absolutely. Yeah.

631
01:29:34,720 --> 01:29:38,720
 And it's fun to see that 10 years later we're here.

632
01:29:38,720 --> 01:29:40,720
 It's finally manifesting.

633
01:29:40,720 --> 01:29:48,720
 Okay. Well, on the subject of predictions, what predictions do you two have for 2026?

634
01:29:48,720 --> 01:29:58,720
 Let me preface this by saying, I asked you this last year, and Eddie, you said that you hoped that all of the new block space would be put toward productive ends.

635
01:29:58,720 --> 01:30:02,720
 We did get meme coins after that. We'll put that aside.

636
01:30:02,720 --> 01:30:09,720
 Darren, you said that you hope that the application section of this report would be longer than the infrastructure section.

637
01:30:09,720 --> 01:30:15,720
 And I think maybe that did come to be the case because we didn't spend too much time talking about infrastructure here.

638
01:30:15,720 --> 01:30:18,720
 We were mostly talking about how people are using this stuff.

639
01:30:18,720 --> 01:30:22,720
 So I think you both were on the money last year with your predictions.

640
01:30:22,720 --> 01:30:25,720
 And I would love to hear what you think is going to happen in 2026.

641
01:30:25,720 --> 01:30:38,720
 Yeah, that's a really interesting question. I mean, yeah, I mean, taking productive charitably, of course, the spirit of my comment last year was that we have all this new capacity, the capacity needs to be used.

642
01:30:38,720 --> 01:30:53,720
 The pattern of the history of technology has been that we figure out how to create some surplus, and then some time passes during which entrepreneurial types figure out how to exploit that surplus and use it for something.

643
01:30:53,720 --> 01:31:00,720
 What they use it for can be difficult to predict, but surpluses don't sit around long like nature doesn't tolerate this.

644
01:31:00,720 --> 01:31:11,720
 So I feel that that has been vindicated to some degree. If I had to predict like the next year to me is where, you know, we continue to see advances in the places we've seen them.

645
01:31:11,720 --> 01:31:18,720
 There will be more infrastructural improvements, more capacity, all those things, you know, we haven't talked too much about that today, but I think that will continue.

646
01:31:18,720 --> 01:31:30,720
 But what I think we will really see is all the integrations and promises and applications and wiring together that we've started to hear about like this year, those will be tested.

647
01:31:30,720 --> 01:31:37,720
 They will launch, they will be live. We will see interesting uses of them, and we will see their flaws as well.

648
01:31:37,720 --> 01:31:45,720
 I alluded to one of them today, which is like, maybe we will see the emperor has no clothes and privacy is actually a very strict prerequisite for many people.

649
01:31:45,720 --> 01:31:52,720
 Maybe maybe we will, maybe we won't, maybe we'll have some Band-Aid solutions while more sophisticated cryptographic solutions come online.

650
01:31:52,720 --> 01:32:03,720
 You know, when financial institutions start putting assets on chain because of the way regulations shake out, maybe some of them will require certain types of credentials, maybe certain types of KYC to access them.

651
01:32:03,720 --> 01:32:05,720
 You know, I don't really know.

652
01:32:05,720 --> 01:32:18,720
 But the point is, as people get to the finish line in launching these new products and showing the culmination of these interests that have been exposed, we will figure out what shortcomings exist.

653
01:32:18,720 --> 01:32:28,720
 And whenever there are obvious bottlenecks, obvious problems in the development of new goods and services, entrepreneurs come and try to fix them.

654
01:32:28,720 --> 01:32:36,720
 And they say, "Okay, you have this huge problem that's blocking you. Okay, I can actually solve that problem for this person, this person, this person, this person."

655
01:32:36,720 --> 01:32:38,720
 And that makes a business.

656
01:32:38,720 --> 01:32:41,720
 So we will start, we will see the evolution of those things.

657
01:32:41,720 --> 01:32:52,720
 I also cautiously think that we will see maybe more applications, maybe that looks more like self-custodial, neo-banky stuff, maybe agentic paymentsy stuff.

658
01:32:52,720 --> 01:33:10,720
 You know, I'm not exactly sure, but my feeling is that as stablecoins get wired up and payments become a possibility and more people have wallets, that it will get easier from a user experience perspective to shift them from like, actually, I only have like a little savings in this thing

659
01:33:10,720 --> 01:33:20,720
 into, "Oh, I can make a payment with it, and I can use this product easily," or "Oh, I can defer to some agent to spend on my behalf," which is controlled by blah, blah, blah, blah.

660
01:33:20,720 --> 01:33:26,720
 I'm not exactly sure, but I think we will see the consequences of the applications and the partnerships.

661
01:33:26,720 --> 01:33:27,720
 Excellent.

662
01:33:27,720 --> 01:33:35,720
 Okay, so we'll see new application areas open up and we'll also be able to identify bottlenecks and shortcomings with the current state of the technology.

663
01:33:35,720 --> 01:33:41,720
 And coupling this with your statement earlier, your earlier bet with Darren on privacy.

664
01:33:41,720 --> 01:33:44,720
 I'm hoping privacy will be a big deal, but we'll see.

665
01:33:44,720 --> 01:33:50,720
 Yeah, people will recognize that we need privacy, but maybe still won't be using it all that much in a year's time.

666
01:33:50,720 --> 01:33:52,720
 All right, Darren, let's hear from you.

667
01:33:52,720 --> 01:33:55,720
 What are your predictions for 2026?

668
01:33:55,720 --> 01:34:02,720
 So I've been saying that crypto is a 17-year-old, so next year it's going to turn 18, which means it's going to be coming.

669
01:34:02,720 --> 01:34:06,720
 It can finally vote, right? A full-on adult.

670
01:34:06,720 --> 01:34:12,720
 And now it's fully legally liable for its own actions.

671
01:34:12,720 --> 01:34:17,720
 And I think that will kind of, I think it'll present itself on the regulatory side, right?

672
01:34:17,720 --> 01:34:22,720
 I think it'll become an adult with kind of real clear rules and regulations.

673
01:34:22,720 --> 01:34:29,720
 I think it'll become an adult with respect to institutional adoption, kind of the commitments will materialize.

674
01:34:29,720 --> 01:34:34,720
 People really will take crypto seriously as an industry.

675
01:34:34,720 --> 01:34:40,720
 And kind of, I hope that just kind of continues from the early signs that we're seeing this year.

676
01:34:40,720 --> 01:34:46,720
 Maybe the one thing I'll say a little bit more specific in terms of predictions this year.

677
01:34:46,720 --> 01:34:55,720
 We've heard a lot of talk about how kind of stablecoins and other things can make things faster or cheaper or better than the traditional way of doing things.

678
01:34:55,720 --> 01:35:03,720
 And while that is all true and we've got a lot of data to support that, I think the real opportunity for bringing the world on chain,

679
01:35:03,720 --> 01:35:08,720
 the real opportunity for kind of rebuilding these global payment rails around stablecoins.

680
01:35:08,720 --> 01:35:14,720
 The real opportunity for crypto is the ability to now do things differently, right?

681
01:35:14,720 --> 01:35:24,720
 Like the experimentation that we will hopefully see around new ways to do payments altogether, things that we're not even thinking about today.

682
01:35:24,720 --> 01:35:31,720
 That's what I'm most excited for, not kind of bringing the fees down from 2% to less than 1%, right?

683
01:35:31,720 --> 01:35:40,720
 Like I think the new opportunities that get unlocked as a result of us kind of rebuilding the financial system, creating this new internet.

684
01:35:40,720 --> 01:35:42,720
 That's what I'm excited for and I hope we start to see that next year.

685
01:35:42,720 --> 01:35:46,720
 Yeah, I think people come for the savings and they stay for the network.

686
01:35:46,720 --> 01:35:48,720
 That's what's really going to happen.

687
01:35:48,720 --> 01:35:58,720
 The consequences of people having custody, having keys, having programmable assets, having composable assets, those are much larger.

688
01:35:58,720 --> 01:36:07,720
 When you say come for the savings, you mean flocking to the sort of store of value narrative and then going from there and saving on transaction fees, saving on payment.

